{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall make its dishes.</b> For kneading in them the showbread that is placed upon it." "<b>And its bowls.</b> For placing in them the frankincense, to put it between the two arrangements of bread." "<b>And its jugs.</b> In which they put water for kneading the dough, as it is written ״ואת קשות הנסך״ — vessels from which they pour water onto the fine flour to knead it. קשות is a term for vessel, as we say: 'one who steals the קשות.'" "<b>And its supports.</b> With which they clean the oven of ashes, and with which they clean the table when they place the bread upon it. <b>And its supports.</b> Rashi explains: split, with six splits at the top, etc. There are many proofs in the portion of the two loaves that the supports prop the sides of the bread so they do not break, and each of the two supports is made in this form, and each split is a handbreadth high and five in width, the measure of the height and width of each side of bread and bread. As they taught there: 'Granted, according to the one who says like an open box — that is why the supports support the bread' — Rashi: two supports called פורק״ש in Old French, standing on the ground next to the table, long and high up to the top bread, and they support the bread so that its sides do not break — 'but according to the one who says like a dancing ship' — Rashi: it has no base; rather it is wide at the top and narrows below, going down to about a finger-breadth, and its two ends are pointed and go upward, and that is why it is called 'dancing,' because it rocks and moves (מהר). — 'How do the supports support the bread?' — Rashi: the sides are made on a slant, and the supports touch only a little at the top of the bread, and cannot support its sides so they not break. — And he answers: 'he bent them in a curve' — Rashi: the supports do not stand upright but stand in a half-circle, what is called צירקל״א, and that curve was doubled beneath the slant of the bread and supports the entire bread, and the head of the support props the head of the bread. And that is why it states 'six splits': according to the one who says like a dancing ship, the bread was made with the upper head of each support attached to the post, and the remainder below was doubled and slanted beneath the bread down to its edge, as Rashi explained. And according to this reading the supports rest upon the table, and most of the discussion below inclines toward the bread being shaped like a dancing ship, and furthermore they would elevate it for the pilgrims and show it to them and say to them: 'See how beloved you are before the Place,' and this matter is difficult to do if it is made like an open box, since the supports must stand on the ground. We learned there: Rabbi Meir says — the table, its length twelve handbreadths and its width six; the showbread, its length ten handbreadths and its width five. The length of the six loaves, one atop the other, corresponds to one of the two ends of the table. He folds one handbreadth of bread here and one handbreadth there, and its width corresponds to the length of the table. He arranges three hollow half-tubes between each loaf and the next, and two on the top one, because there is none above it, and stands two supports on the ground next to the table, one on this side and one on that, long and high above the top bread, and each one is split into six splits, aligned to insert in them the tubes between loaf and loaf, to support with them the weight of the bread and its sides so they do not break. It turns out that in one arrangement there are six loaves and fourteen tubes and two supports; this is the order of one arrangement. And in this order he would set the second arrangement — the other six loaves — at the other end of the table; it turns out there is a handbreadth of wind in the middle between the two arrangements along the length of the table, so that the wind blows through them. Abba Shaul says: there they would place two bowls of frankincense, and that is what is written ״ונתת על המערכת לבונה זכה אצל המערכת״." "<b>With which it is covered.</b> It refers to its jugs."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {3 "The comment embeds extended Rashi citations within the Talmudic discussion (Menahot 94a–b). The Old French glosses פורק״ש and צירקל״א are left untranslated as in the source. The phrase 'רוב השטה דלהלן' (reading שטה = discussion) is textually uncertain; translated as 'most of the discussion below.' The editorial parenthetical '(מהר)' is rendered '(מהר)' as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:46.531575716Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}